  "text": "In the present study, one of the most significant findings was that the knowledge about CKD had no relation to income, where Participants with the highest income had the lowest knowledge levels 51.2%, we think that this may be attributed to the better healthcare access and screening among well-off population, on the contrary the Saudi study [14] found a link between higher levels of knowledge and higher economic position. Furthermore, our study suggested a positive correlation between the adequate level of knowledge among southern residents in Riyadh 63.4%, this might be related to the presence of Al Iman General Hospital and its health awareness activities in that area. These figures are broadly comparable with those reported by Assiry et al. [14], who surveyed 440 residents of Jazan Province and found that only 27.3% demonstrated good knowledge of CKD, 7.5% a high level of awareness of its risk factors and 9.3% a high level of awareness of its complications, with knowledge being significantly associated with being a student or employee (χ2 = 29.90; p < 0.001), having completed graduate studies (χ2 = 63.86; p < 0.001) and residing in an urban area (χ2 = 138.62; p < 0.001)."
}

